 "id": "cases/evolve-jintrac-iter-15ma-flattop",
 "type": "fyo:ScenarioSpecification",
 "title": {
  "zh": "JINTRAC 15 MA 平顶段 · ITER 作业 102530 · 83.51–99.83 s（参考数据在 fydoc）",
  "en": "JINTRAC 15 MA flat-top - ITER job 102530, 83.51-99.83 s (reference data lives in fydoc)"
 },
 "note": {
  "zh": "★★这一档是本仓第一次拿 **JINTRAC 运行自己的输出**当参考，而不是拿一份 PDF 里的表格。值得收的理由是它带着**度规**：`equilibrium.h5` 里有 `dvolume_drho_tor` / `gm2` / `gm3` / `f` / `psi` / `q`，所以对照可以把几何这一项**摘掉**，只剩输运——只给剖面的话，任何分歧都可以推给一个谁也验不了的几何。\n★★**结论是：复现没有成立。** 拿运行自己的度规、把闭包常数取成参考自己的 chi 的体平均（chi0 = 1.371 是 <chi_i>，chi_ratio = 0.525 = <chi_e>/<chi_i>，读来的不是拧的），收敛到 1280 步之后，T_e 全剖面 RMS **41.6 %**，而「什么都不做」（把 t0 剖面当答案）只有 **4.07 %**。误差整个堆在外半（x > 0.5 处 RMS 58.6 %，最差 +94 % @ x = 0.92），内半 4.3 % 勉强追平零假设。把 chi0 扫 35 倍，常数闭包能做到的**最好**是 30.8 % —— 仍是零假设的 7.6 倍。所以这不是常数没调好，是**常数闭包做不到**：参考自己的 chi_e 从内半 0.32 升到外半 0.87 m²/s，一个常数按不住两头。全部量在 `tests/test_jintrac_flattop.py`，来龙去脉写在 `docs/note/jintrac-flattop-reproduction.md`。\n★★**这份配置不是那次复现**。配置里的 `geometry: miller` 是本仓这条控制路径能表示的形状，度规是按 Miller 参数重建的；定量复现走 Python 侧、用运行自己的度规。两者不要混为一谈。\n★★追因的结果写在 `docs/note/jintrac-flattop-reproduction.md` §8b：gap 是**三笔**（闭包、锯齿、规定道），而 χ 的**定义**查过、是对的——本仓的通量律让参考自己的数据自洽到 0.90，另两种候选差 1.3–1.9 倍。\n★★这一档**本仓跑不了**，`fylite cases --plan` 会把缺口逐条报出来，头一条是 TGLF 闭包。但最要紧的缺口 `--plan` **报不出来**（它只认页面上有的控件）：参考侧只解电流和电子温度，T_i 与 n_e 是 ex-file **给定**的。本仓没有「把一道钉在给定时序上」的模式，于是本仓的 T_i 是预测、参考的 T_i 是输入——**只有 T_e 是两边都预测的那一道**，其余的比较必须带着这句话读。\n★形状控制是**对参考剖面拟合出来的**，不是估的：`peakt` 取 Te 的拟合指数 2.40（Ti 拟合出 2.04，一个控制只能取一个，取两边都预测的那道），拟合残差对轴值 2.4 % / 1.5 %；`peakn` 是 ne 的 0.67（残差 4.1 %）；`dep` / `depw` 是 NBI 沉积剖面的高斯拟合 0.05 / 0.37（残差 10 % 峰值，沉积重心 <rho> = 0.258）。`cimp` 1.008 % 是由实测 Zeff 1.121 按铍（Z = 4）反推的：(Zeff − 1) / (Z² − Z)。\n★`beam` 关着，是因为**这份运行目录里读不到束的切向半径**（PINI 几何在机器侧的文件里，不在 `jetto.in`）。所以本仓不去编一个 `beamrtan`，束的那组旋钮留在缺省值、不起作用，而参考实际用的 PENCIL 执行器写在 `caveat` 里。配置里的 `pe` / `pi` 是参考**实测的**电子/离子分配（14.4 / 1.91 MW），不是本仓分的。\n★`nlev` 51 是运行自己的 `NGRID`；它的 IDS 把剖面写成 50 点，两个数都照记。`nsteps` 160 是**页面这条路径的**步数，不是复现用的步数——复现要 1280 步才收敛，160 步会给出一个好看但没收敛的数（第一版正是这么报的）。\n★这次运行的 `jetto.eqdsk_out` **被 `measure_cocos` 拒收**（残差 0.842、领先倍数 1.59），所以 `geometry` 不能取 `gfile`；原因未定，排除过程见那份笔记。",
  "en": "★★This is the first case in this repository whose reference is a JINTRAC run's OWN OUTPUT rather than a table in a PDF. It is worth keeping because it carries the METRIC: `equilibrium.h5` states `dvolume_drho_tor`, `gm2`, `gm3`, `f`, `psi` and `q`, so a comparison can take GEOMETRY OUT and be left with transport -- given profiles alone, any disagreement can always be blamed on a geometry nobody can check.\n★★THE REPRODUCTION DOES NOT HOLD. Given the run's own metric, with the closure constant READ from the reference's own chi (chi0 = 1.371 = <chi_i>, chi_ratio = 0.525 = <chi_e>/<chi_i>) rather than tuned, and converged to 1280 steps, T_e is 41.6 % RMS off over the profile -- against 4.07 % for doing nothing at all (carrying t0's profile forward). The error is all in the outer half (58.6 % RMS for x > 0.5, worst +94 % at x = 0.92); the inner half's 4.3 % barely matches the null. Sweeping chi0 over a factor of 35, the BEST a constant closure reaches is 30.8 % -- still 7.6x the null. So this is not a badly chosen constant, it is that A CONSTANT CLOSURE CANNOT DO IT: the reference's own chi_e rises from 0.32 to 0.87 m^2/s between the inner and outer half, and one constant cannot hold both ends. Every number is measured in `tests/test_jintrac_flattop.py`; the account is in `docs/note/jintrac-flattop-reproduction.md`.\n★★THIS CONFIG IS NOT THAT REPRODUCTION. `geometry: miller` here is the shape this repository's control path can express, with the metric rebuilt from Miller parameters; the quantitative reproduction runs on the Python side against the run's own metric.\n★★The causes are traced in `docs/note/jintrac-flattop-reproduction.md` §8b: the gap is THREE things (the closure, the sawtooth, the prescribed channel), and chi's DEFINITION was checked and is right — this repository's flux law closes the reference's own data at 0.90 where the two alternatives are 1.3-1.9x off.\n★★This repository CANNOT run this case; `fylite cases --plan` reports the gaps by name and the first is the TGLF closure. But the gap that matters most is one `--plan` CANNOT report (it only knows the controls the page has): the reference solves current and electron temperature only, and T_i and n_e are GIVEN by the ex-file. This repository has no mode that pins a channel to a given trace, so its T_i is a prediction and the reference's T_i is an input -- ELECTRON TEMPERATURE IS THE ONLY CHANNEL BOTH SIDES PREDICT, and every other comparison must be read with that sentence attached.\n★The shape controls are FITTED to the reference's own profiles, not estimated: `peakt` is Te's fitted exponent 2.40 (Ti fits 2.04; one control takes one value, so it takes the channel both sides predict), residuals 2.4 % / 1.5 % of the axis value; `peakn` is ne's 0.67 (4.1 %); `dep` / `depw` are a Gaussian fit to the NBI deposition profile, 0.05 / 0.37 (10 % of peak; deposition centroid <rho> = 0.258). `cimp` 1.008 % follows from the measured Zeff 1.121 for beryllium (Z = 4): (Zeff - 1) / (Z^2 - Z).\n★`beam` is off because THE BEAM'S TANGENCY RADIUS IS NOT READABLE FROM THIS RUN DIRECTORY (the PINI geometry lives in machine-side files, not `jetto.in`). Rather than invent a `beamrtan`, the beam knobs stay at their defaults and inert, and the PENCIL executor the reference actually used is named in `caveat`. The config's `pe` / `pi` are the reference's MEASURED electron/ion split (14.4 / 1.91 MW), not this repository's.\n★`nlev` 51 is the run's own `NGRID`; its IDS writes the profiles on 50 points. `nsteps` 160 is THIS PATH's step count, not the reproduction's -- that needs 1280 steps to converge, and 160 gives a flattering but unconverged answer (which is exactly what the first reading of this comparison reported).\n★This run's `jetto.eqdsk_out` is REFUSED by `measure_cocos` (residual 0.842, margin 1.59), so `geometry` cannot be `gfile`. The cause is undetermined; what was ruled out is in the note."
 },
 "prescribed_task_kind": "fyo:PhysicsModelingTask",
 "prescribes_code": {
